                    ALGEBRAIC NUMBER THEORY

This directory is a library of algebraic number theory: Dedekind domains,
number fields and their rings of integers, ideal class groups, the
splitting of primes, discriminants and differents, the Hilbert theory of
Galois extensions, units and S-units, Minkowski theory, quadratic and
cyclotomic fields, absolute values and places, and the Dedekind zeta
function with the Frobenius density theorem.

Load all of it with

  loadt "ANT/make.ml";;

or just one file, together with what it needs, with

  needs "ANT/x.ml";;

Each file starts with needs lines for its prerequisites. The files before
the needs line of Multivariate/cauchy.ml in ANT/make.ml use only files of
Library/; the others also use the complex numbers (Multivariate/cauchy.ml,
for the fundamental theorem of algebra), and the files on absolute values,
the Dedekind zeta function and densities also need
Multivariate/transcendentals.ml or Multivariate/realanalysis.ml. The files
contain no priority settings and load under any of prioritize_num(),
prioritize_int(), prioritize_real() and prioritize_complex() (the library
files they need leave one of these). From a fresh hol.ml, loadt
"ANT/make.ml" takes about 56 minutes of CPU time: the files before the
needs line of Multivariate/cauchy.ml about 18 minutes with the library
files they load, Multivariate/cauchy.ml and its prerequisites about 35
minutes, and the rest of ANT about 3 minutes.

CONVENTIONS

Number fields are abstract: number_field k for a ring k : A ring means
that k is a field of characteristic 0 that is a finite extension of its
prime subfield Q = subfield_generated k {ring_0 k}. Nothing requires k to
be a subfield of the complex numbers; embeddings into C are a separate
notion (complex_embeddings k). The ring of integers ring_of_integers k is
the subring of the elements integral over Z, that is over integer_ring
along ring_of_int k.

Extensions are given by ring homomorphisms, as in Library/fieldtheory.ml
and Library/galois.ml: an extension of number fields is (k,l) h with
field k, number_field l and ring_homomorphism(k,l) h. The relative notions
take these arguments (number_field_different (k,l) h,
number_field_ideal_norm (k,l) h, ramification_index (r,s) h P,
residue_degree (r,s) h P, decomposition_group (k,l) h b Q, ...), and the
absolute ones are their instances over the prime subfield,
(subfield_generated k {ring_0 k},k) I. Theorems carry the plain names in
the general form. An absolute form is a separate theorem only when it is
not a one-step instance of the general one; it then has a suffix naming
the instantiation, _Q for the base Q (or Z) and _IN_C for subfields of C.

Dedekind domains are treated in general (dedekind_domain r), with
valuations dedekind_valuation (r,k) h P on a field of fractions k of r
along h (field_of_fractions (r,k) h); the ideal norm ideal_norm r j is the
cardinality of r/j, for any ring.

Dedekind domains include fields; DVR excludes fields. Fractional ideals
are nonzero, and fractional_ideal (r,k) h J includes the condition
field_of_fractions (r,k) h. The valuation functions have their intended
meaning at nonzero prime ideals and nonzero elements or ideals. Their
definitions use choice, so values outside the stated hypotheses are
unspecified. In particular, dedekind_valuation does not represent the
value infinity at zero. The numerical ideal norm is used as an index
when the quotient is finite; CARD has no cardinality interpretation for
infinite quotients.

An infinite place is the absolute value x |-> norm(g x), one for each
real embedding or pair of conjugate complex embeddings. The local
weights are 1 and 2 respectively. The product formula uses all complex
embeddings, and the regulator uses the local weights on infinite places,
giving the usual factor 2 on the complex logarithms.

At a finite prime P the product formula uses N(P)^(-v_P(x)).
The absolute values extended to Q-bar in valuation_rank_one.ml instead
normalize |p| to 1/p, using p^(-v_P(x)/e_P) on finite subfields.
These are the usual two normalizations; their powers differ by the local
degree e_P f_P.

An integer lattice is a subgroup of the integer coordinate vectors
supported on an index set s. The finite-dimensional lattice results
assume FINITE s.

For a ring extension h : r -> s and an ideal p of r, unramified (r,s) h
p is the textbook notion (Neukirch, Algebraic Number Theory, I.8): every
prime P of s containing h(p) has ramification index 1 and a separable
residue extension, s/P over the image of r. This residue ring is a field
only for maximal P, so the condition fails at a prime above p that is not
maximal; above the nonzero primes of rings of integers every such P is
maximal. weakly_unramified (r,s) h p asks only for ramification index 1
(weakly unramified in the Stacks Project, Definition 09E4; Example 09EX
has e = 1 and an inseparable residue extension). Both hold vacuously when
no prime contains h(p). An unramified ideal is weakly unramified, and the
two agree when the residue rings above p are finite
(UNRAMIFIED_EQ_WEAKLY_UNRAMIFIED_FINITE) or when p is maximal with r/p
perfect and the residue extensions are algebraic
(UNRAMIFIED_EQ_WEAKLY_UNRAMIFIED_PERFECT); so they agree for the nonzero
primes of rings of integers of number fields
(NUMBER_FIELD_UNRAMIFIED_EQ_WEAKLY_UNRAMIFIED, its form _Q over Z, and
RING_OF_INTEGERS_UNRAMIFIED_EQ_WEAKLY_UNRAMIFIED). Results on general
ring extensions are stated with weakly_unramified, those on number fields
with unramified. The definitions of the splitting types and their
conventions are detailed in splitting_types.ml.

A Galois extension is assumed as galois_extension(k,l) h alone (with
number_field l for extensions of number fields): it implies field k,
field l and ring_homomorphism(k,l) h (GALOIS_IMP_FIELD_EXTENSION), as in
Library/galois.ml.

The set maximal_unramified_outside (k,l) h S is the union of finite
Galois subextensions of the supplied ambient field l unramified outside
S. It is a subfield of l, Galois over k for every ambient field l
(GALOIS_MAXIMAL_UNRAMIFIED_OUTSIDE). Taking l to be an algebraic closure
gives the usual maximal extension. For an arbitrary ambient field the
definition selects the subextensions that lie in such Galois
subextensions. Ramification at infinite places is unrestricted.

The zeta series converges for complex s with Re s > 1. The estimates
near s = 1 concern real s tending to 1 from above; this library proves
bounds of order 1 / (s - 1) and logarithmic estimates. Analytic
continuation and the simple pole are further results. The usual
interpretation of has_dirichlet_density k S d assumes that S consists of
nonzero prime ideals; its definition does not impose that condition or
summability. The elementary laws of density
(HAS_DIRICHLET_DENSITY_BOUNDS, _SUBSET, _DISJOINT_UNION, _DIFF_SUBSET,
_COMPLEMENT, _FINITE_SYMDIFF) assume sets of nonzero prime ideals. The
Frobenius density theorem counts primes whose Frobenius generates a
specified conjugate cyclic subgroup. This is the classical weak density
theorem.

CONTENTS

The sections of ANT/make.ml, in load order:

  Generic material: unique factorization domains, integral elements and
  integral closure (the determinant trick, integrally closed domains,
  minimal polynomials, traces, norms and units over them), lying over and
  going up, ideal arithmetic (products, quotients, the Chinese remainder
  theorem, Nakayama), the Hensel lemma at finite level.

  Dedekind domains: factorization of ideals, valuations, discrete
  valuation rings, two generators, ideal classes, localization, the
  valuations of the fraction field, valuation rings and the Chevalley
  extension theorem, the ideal norm, Euler and Fermat for ideals, absolute
  values and the P-adic absolute values.

  Integer lattices, the Minkowski linear forms theorem and the Hermite
  inequality for positive definite quadratic forms.

  Number fields: the integral closure of a Dedekind domain in a finite
  separable extension, rings of integers and integral bases, the norm of
  principal ideals, finiteness of the class group, valuations of elements,
  approximation, fractional ideals and their group, characterizations of
  Dedekind domains.

  Splitting of primes and the discriminant: e(P|p), f(P|p) and the
  fundamental identity, in towers and relative extensions, the
  discriminant and the index formula, ramification, the different and its
  exponents, the Dedekind-Kummer theorem, Eisenstein polynomials, quadratic
  fields and their decomposition law.

  Hilbert theory: splitting types (unramified and weakly unramified
  ideals), decomposition and inertia groups, Frobenius elements, the
  decomposition and inertia fields, the Artin symbol of abelian
  extensions, conjugate ideals, infinitely many totally split primes, the
  relative ideal norm and discriminant, maps of class groups, rings of
  S-integers and their class groups, extensions unramified outside a set
  of primes, valuation subrings of number fields and of Q-bar.

  Finitely generated abelian groups: extensions, cosets of the m-th
  powers, a discreteness criterion, independent sets of maximal size.

  Cyclotomic fields: cyclotomic polynomials over any ring, the degree, the
  ring of integers and the discriminant, the decomposition law, the Galois
  group, Frobenius elements, subfields and cyclotomic units, the quadratic
  subfield through Gauss sums.

  Embeddings into C: complex conjugation and the cyclotomic character,
  finite generation of units and S-units, finiteness of K(S,m), infinite
  places, Minkowski theory (the class bound sqrt|d_K|, |d_K| > 1 for
  k <> Q), the Galois group of a Galois extension of Q generated by
  inertia, composita with coprime discriminants, the Hermite finiteness
  theorem, the Dirichlet unit theorem, the logarithmic embedding and the
  regulator, roots of unity, quadratic units and the Pell equation, the
  Stickelberger theorem.

  Absolute values: the product formula, the S-unit theorem with the rank,
  valuations of rank one, equivalence of absolute values, the
  approximation theorem of Artin and Whaples, the Ostrowski theorem for Q
  and the number fields of degree 1, the nonarchimedean places of number
  fields.

  Higher ramification groups, local power bases and the Euler formula for
  the different, the Hilbert formula for the different.

  The Dedekind zeta function and Dirichlet densities: the number of
  ideals of bounded norm, the Euler product, bounds near s = 1, the
  elementary laws of density, the Frobenius density theorem.

SOME MAIN THEOREMS

  DEDEKIND_IDEAL_FACTORIZATION       unique factorization of ideals
  DEDEKIND_INTEGRAL_CLOSURE          integral closure in a finite
                                     separable extension is Dedekind
  VALUATION_SUBRING_DOMINATES        Chevalley extension theorem
  DEDEKIND_EQ_IDEALS_INVERTIBLE      Dedekind iff every ideal invertible
  IDEAL_EULER, IDEAL_NORM_FERMAT     Euler and Fermat for ideals
  HENSEL_LEMMA                       Hensel lifting modulo powers
  DEDEKIND_RING_OF_INTEGERS          the ring of integers is Dedekind
  NUMBER_FIELD_CLASS_GROUP_FINITE    finiteness of the class group
  NUMBER_FIELD_FUNDAMENTAL_IDENTITY  sum of e f is the degree
  NUMBER_FIELD_RAMIFIED_DISCRIMINANT p ramifies iff p divides d_K
  NUMBER_FIELD_DEDEKIND_KUMMER       Dedekind-Kummer over O_k
  NUMBER_FIELD_DIFFERENT_TOWER       the different in towers
  NUMBER_FIELD_DISCRIMINANT_TOWER    the discriminant in towers
  NUMBER_FIELD_DIFFERENT_HILBERT_FORMULA
                                     v_Q(D) = sum (|G_i| - 1)
  NUMBER_FIELD_EISENSTEIN            Eisenstein implies totally ramified
  NUMBER_FIELD_UNRAMIFIED_EQ_WEAKLY_UNRAMIFIED
                                     unramified iff e = 1 (number fields)
  NUMBER_FIELD_FROBENIUS_ELEMENT     Frobenius elements
  NUMBER_FIELD_TOTALLY_SPLIT_INFINITE
                                     infinitely many totally split primes
  GALOIS_MAXIMAL_UNRAMIFIED_OUTSIDE  K_S is Galois over k
  NUMBER_FIELD_IDEAL_MINKOWSKI       Minkowski theorem for ideals
  NUMBER_FIELD_DISCRIMINANT_ABS_GT_1 |d_K| > 1 for k <> Q
  NUMBER_FIELD_INERTIA_GROUPS_GENERATE
                                     Gal(L/Q) generated by inertia
  NUMBER_FIELD_HERMITE               finitely many fields of a given degree
                                     and bounded discriminant
  NUMBER_FIELD_FUNDAMENTAL_UNITS     Dirichlet unit theorem
  S_UNIT_GROUP_FUNDAMENTAL           S-unit theorem with the rank
  NUMBER_FIELD_REGULATOR             the regulator
  NUMBER_FIELD_DISCRIMINANT_MOD_4    Stickelberger: d_K = 0, 1 (mod 4)
  PELL_EQUATION_INFINITE             the Pell equation
  CYCLOTOMIC_FIELD_RING_OF_INTEGERS  O = Z[z] for cyclotomic fields
  CYCLOTOMIC_FIELD_GALOIS_GROUP      Gal(Q(z)/Q) = (Z/n)^*
  QUADRATIC_GAUSS_SUM                g^2 = p* for the Gauss sum
  ABSOLUTE_VALUE_WEAK_APPROXIMATION  Artin-Whaples approximation
  OSTROWSKI                          the absolute values of a number
                                     field of degree 1
  OSTROWSKI_RATIONAL                 the absolute values of Q
  NUMBER_FIELD_PRODUCT_FORMULA       the product formula
  DEDEKIND_ZETA_EULER_PRODUCT        Euler product of the zeta function
  NUMBER_FIELD_FROBENIUS_DENSITY     the Frobenius density theorem

RELATED FILES OUTSIDE THIS DIRECTORY

Most of the general lemmas that ANT needs are in the library files; the
header of each ANT file names the library files and the results it uses.
The most closely related files:

  Library/ringtheory.ml, fieldtheory.ml, ring_det.ml, galois.ml,
  cyclotomic.ml, finite_fields.ml   the algebra ANT builds on
  Library/normal_basis.ml           the normal basis theorem
  Multivariate/complex_algebra.ml   the algebra of the complex numbers,
                                    without topology
  Multivariate/complex_ring.ml      the complex numbers as a ring
  Multivariate/complex_field.ml     C is algebraically closed
  Multivariate/profinite_groups.ml  topological and profinite groups
  Multivariate/infinite_galois.ml   the Krull topology and the infinite
                                    Galois correspondence

REFERENCES

The comments refer to these books for statements and proofs:

  S. Alaca and K. S. Williams, "Introductory Algebraic Number Theory",
  Cambridge University Press 2004.
  M. F. Atiyah and I. G. Macdonald, "Introduction to Commutative Algebra",
  Addison-Wesley 1969.
  S. Lang, "Algebraic Number Theory", 2nd edition, Springer 1994.
  D. A. Marcus, "Number Fields", Springer 1977.
  H. Matsumura, "Commutative Ring Theory", Cambridge University Press 1986.
  W. Narkiewicz, "Elementary and Analytic Theory of Algebraic Numbers",
  3rd edition, Springer 2004.
  J. Neukirch, "Algebraic Number Theory", Springer 1999.
  P. Ribenboim, "Classical Theory of Algebraic Numbers", Springer 2001.
  J.-P. Serre, "Local Fields", Springer 1979.

